//! lotus/flanger.rye -- ALES73: the flanger -- a short modulated delay with a FEEDBACK path. The chorus (ALES71) and vibrato (ALES72) both
//! read a MODULATED delay off a FROZEN snapshot of the dry, precisely so a moving read never reads a sample it just wrote. The echo (ALES66)
//! does the opposite: it reads the LIVE buffer, so each output feeds the next and the train decays. The FLANGER is where the two meet -- a
//! short modulated delay whose delayed voice is FED BACK into itself. The moving comb it makes, its notches sliding up and down and deepened
//! by the feedback, is the jet-plane whoosh a keeper reaches for by name. It is the family's first rung that adds a GENUINELY NEW ELEMENT
//! rather than only changing the mix: the vibrato and chorus only chose what reached the output; the flanger adds a feedback path.
//!
//! The crux is that A MODULATED FRACTIONAL DELAY IS READ OFF THE LIVE OUTPUT. This reuses ALES71's triangle_delay_at driver and ALES70's
//! interpolation read, and reuses ALES66's discipline that THE DELAY LINE IS THE CLIP BUFFER ITSELF. Over one period P (a positive multiple
//! of four, q = P/4) with phase p = k % P, d_s(k) = centre_scaled + triangle(depth_scaled, P, k); then near = buf[i − d_whole] (a prior
//! OUTPUT -- feedback -- or dry before the span, silence before the clip), far = buf[i − d_whole − 1], delayed = (near-(glide_scale − frac) +
//! far-frac)/glide_scale, and y[i] = saturate(x[i] + fb-delayed). Because the delay never dips below one whole sample (centre − depth ≥
//! glide_scale, so d_whole ≥ 1), both neighbours sit at indices STRICTLY BEFORE i -- already written this pass (true feedback), or dry before
//! the span, or silence before the clip. So the read is off the LIVE buffer, not a snapshot: the flanger WANTS to hear its own output, and
//! reading strictly-earlier indices keeps that safe in place -- the exact inversion of the chorus/vibrato snapshot, and the reason the flanger
//! needs none. Two facts keep it bounded, both from the echo: `delayed` is a convex combination of two i16 neighbours so it fits the rail with
//! no snapshot, and FEEDBACK BELOW UNITY DECAYS AND THE SUM SATURATES ONCE -- num < den is refused above that (BadFeedback, a runaway), each
//! fed term strictly smaller than the output it read, a constructive sum past the rail saturating rather than wrapping. fb = 0 is the dry
//! identity, and a ZERO-DEPTH flanger is exactly ALES66's echo at its centre byte-for-byte (the periodic driver reduces to the fixed one at
//! rest, the interpolation exact at the whole delay).
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES73.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-200314_fill-ales73-lotus-flanger-feedback-modulated-delay.md
//! Stands on: lotus/chorus.rye (ALES71) -- the triangle-LFO driver `triangle_delay_at`; lotus/echo.rye (ALES66) -- the feedback delay whose
//! in-place delay-line-is-the-buffer idiom this reuses, and which a zero-depth flanger equals byte-for-byte; lotus/glide.rye (ALES70) -- the
//! `glide_scale` fixed-point resolution and the interpolation read; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, sample_min/max, max_clip, saturate.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM in one clip, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate -- the delay is counted in scaled sample indices and the period in samples, not milliseconds or
//! hertz (the real-time twin follows as echo_ms and multitap_ms did). No lookahead beyond the in-place feedback read; one delayed fractional
//! read and one multiply-divide per sample, the sum in i64 saturating once, so nothing on the path can overflow.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/flanger.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const glide = @import("glide.rye");
const chorus = @import("chorus.rye");

pub const flanger_version = "20260814.200314";

/// The flanger's faults, one consistent name each: a period that is not a positive multiple of four (the triangle's quarter anchors need
/// P/4 whole) or wider than max_clip, a swing that would dip below one whole sample (centre − depth < glide_scale) or reach past the widest
/// clip (centre + depth whole > max_clip), a feedback at or above unity (a runaway that would never decay) or with a zero denominator, and a
/// span outside the samples. Each refused before any write.
pub const FlangerError = error{
    /// A period that is not a positive multiple of four, or wider than max_clip -- refused, no write.
    BadPeriod,
    /// A negative depth, or a swing whose bottom (centre − depth) falls under one whole sample -- refused, no write.
    BadDepth,
    /// A swing whose top (centre + depth) whole delay is longer than max_clip samples -- refused, no write.
    BadDelay,
    /// A feedback numerator at or above the denominator (would never decay -- a runaway) or a zero denominator -- refused, no write.
    BadFeedback,
    /// A span outside the current samples -- refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Apply a flanger over `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip`: a triangle LFO of `depth_scaled` round `centre_scaled` over
/// `period` samples drives the scaled delay (ALES71's `triangle_delay_at`), each sample read by LINEAR INTERPOLATION of the two neighbours
/// straddling its fractional delay (ALES70's read) off the LIVE buffer (ALES66's feedback -- the delay line is the clip buffer itself), then
/// fed back at `fb_num/fb_den` -- y[i] = saturate(x[i] + fb-interp(buf, i, d_s(i))). Because the delay is at least one whole sample the two
/// neighbours sit strictly before i (already-written outputs, dry, or silence), so the in-place read hears the flanger's own output safely --
/// no snapshot. Feedback below unity decays; a constructive sum past the rail saturates once. `fb_num == 0` is the dry identity, and a
/// zero-depth flanger equals ALES66's echo at its centre byte-for-byte. Refuses BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadFeedback, and BadRange
/// before any write; on refusal the clip is untouched. Values only -- never a length.
/// Validate a flanger's LFO, feedback, and span against a clip length ONCE, before any write -- factored out of `flanger` so the stereo lift
/// (`stereo_flanger`, ALES180) can refuse against the shared length before either channel is touched, mirroring ALES71's `chorus.precheck`,
/// ALES72's `vibrato.precheck`, and ALES75's `ring_mod.precheck`. Refuses a period not a positive multiple of four (or wider than max_clip)
/// BadPeriod, a negative depth or a swing dipping below one whole sample BadDepth, a swing top past the widest clip BadDelay, a zero denominator
/// or a feedback at or above unity (a runaway) BadFeedback, and a span outside `clip_len` BadRange.
pub fn precheck(clip_len: u32, start: u32, count: u32, centre_scaled: i64, depth_scaled: i64, period: u32, fb_num: u32, fb_den: u32) FlangerError!void {
    // invariant: the period is a positive multiple of four (the triangle's quarter anchors need P/4 whole) and no wider than the widest
    // clip, so the LFO is bounded. Checked at the edge before any read.
    if (period == 0 or period % 4 != 0) return error.BadPeriod;
    if (period > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadPeriod;
    // invariant: the depth is non-negative and the swing's bottom stays at or above one whole sample -- below that the near neighbour would
    // fall on i itself (a self-read, not feedback), and there is no `far` neighbour before it. This is what makes the live read safe.
    if (depth_scaled < 0) return error.BadDepth;
    if (centre_scaled - depth_scaled < glide.glide_scale) return error.BadDepth;
    // invariant: the swing's top whole delay fits the widest clip, so the reach is bounded.
    if (@divTrunc(centre_scaled + depth_scaled, glide.glide_scale) > @as(i64, timeline.max_clip)) return error.BadDelay;
    // invariant: the feedback is a legal fraction strictly below unity -- a nonzero denominator, a numerator under it, so each echo is
    // strictly smaller than the last and the loop always decays. fb_num == 0 (the dry identity) is legal.
    if (fb_den == 0) return error.BadFeedback;
    if (fb_num >= fb_den) return error.BadFeedback;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples -- checked without underflow (count vs the remainder).
    if (start > clip_len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip_len - start) return error.BadRange;
}

pub fn flanger(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    centre_scaled: i64,
    depth_scaled: i64,
    period: u32,
    fb_num: u32,
    fb_den: u32,
) FlangerError!void {
    // invariant: the LFO, feedback, and span are all legal -- the five faults refused before any read, so a refusal never half-writes.
    // ALES180's stereo lift reuses this same `precheck` against the shared length.
    try precheck(clip.len, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period, fb_num, fb_den);

    const scale: i64 = glide.glide_scale;
    const num: i64 = @as(i64, fb_num);
    const den: i64 = @as(i64, fb_den);
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        const x: i64 = @as(i64, clip.buf[i]); // the dry input at this sample (not yet overwritten)
        const d_s: i64 = chorus.triangle_delay_at(centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period, k);
        // invariant: the LFO never leaves the validated delay window, so the whole delay stays at least one sample and within the clip.
        assert(d_s >= scale and @divTrunc(d_s, scale) <= @as(i64, timeline.max_clip));
        const d_whole: i64 = @divTrunc(d_s, scale);
        const frac: i64 = d_s - d_whole * scale; // in [0, glide_scale)
        // invariant: the fractional part is a legal weight strictly under the scale.
        assert(frac >= 0 and frac < scale);
        // invariant: the whole delay is at least one sample, so both neighbours sit strictly before i -- the read hears prior OUTPUT
        // (feedback), never the sample being written. This is the safety the BadDepth check buys.
        assert(d_whole >= 1);
        // The two neighbours straddling the fractional delay, read off the LIVE buffer: `near` at the smaller delay (a prior output inside
        // the span -- true feedback -- or dry before it), `far` one sample further back. Each is silence before the clip (index < 0).
        const near_ix: i64 = @as(i64, i) - d_whole;
        const far_ix: i64 = near_ix - 1;
        const near_val: i64 = if (near_ix >= 0) @as(i64, clip.buf[@intCast(near_ix)]) else 0;
        const far_val: i64 = if (far_ix >= 0) @as(i64, clip.buf[@intCast(far_ix)]) else 0;
        // invariant: both neighbours already fit the i16 rail -- each is a written output (saturated) or dry audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(near_val >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and near_val <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        assert(far_val >= @as(i64, timeline.sample_min) and far_val <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        // Linear interpolation (ALES70's read): near weighted by (scale − frac), far by frac, divided once. frac = 0 returns near exactly
        // (a whole-sample delay is an exact integer feedback tap -- the zero-depth echo equality). |near|,|far| <= 32768 and the weights sum
        // to glide_scale, so the product fits i64.
        const delayed: i64 = @divTrunc(near_val * (scale - frac) + far_val * frac, scale);
        // The feedback term at fb = num/den (num < den, so |fed| < |delayed|), then the dry plus fed saturates ONCE over the true sum.
        const fed: i64 = @divTrunc(num * delayed, den);
        // invariant: the fed-back term is strictly smaller in magnitude than the delayed read -- feedback below unity only shrinks it, so
        // the loop always decays.
        assert((if (fed < 0) -fed else fed) <= (if (delayed < 0) -delayed else delayed));
        clip.buf[i] = timeline.saturate(x + fed);
    }
    // invariant: a flanger changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: fb = 0 is the dry identity; a ZERO-DEPTH flanger is EXACTLY ALES66's echo at its centre byte-for-byte (the periodic driver
//     reduces to the fixed one at rest); a hand-computed modulated-FEEDBACK read matches exactly (the read is off the live output); a loud
//     constructive sum SATURATES rather than wraps; and each fault refuses by name, the clip untouched -----------------------------------

const echo = @import("echo.rye");

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const scale = glide.glide_scale;

    // 1. fb = 0 IS THE DRY IDENTITY -- a zero feedback numerator adds nothing, the dry signal passes byte-for-byte at any modulation.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1000, -2000, 3000, -4000, 5000, -6000, 7000, -8000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 3 * scale, 1 * scale, 8, 0, 4); // fb 0/4, the feedback turned fully off
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.ZeroFbNotIdentity;
    }

    // 2. A ZERO-DEPTH FLANGER IS EXACTLY ALES66's ECHO AT ITS CENTRE -- depth == 0, the LFO at rest, a fixed whole delay of `centre`: the
    //    interpolation returns the nearer sample exactly (frac 0), so the flanger equals echo(centre, fb) byte-for-byte (both tools run --
    //    the periodic driver reduces to the fixed one at rest). An impulse exercises the whole decaying tail.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 8000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        var flanged = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&flanged, &sig);
        try flanger(&flanged, 0, flanged.len, 2 * scale, 0, 8, 1, 2); // centre delay 2, fb 1/2, depth 0
        var echoed = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&echoed, &sig);
        try echo.echo(&echoed, 0, echoed.len, 2, 1, 2); // the same fixed feedback delay
        if (!eq(flanged.samples(), echoed.samples())) return error.ZeroDepthNotEcho;
    }

    // 3. A HAND-COMPUTED MODULATED-FEEDBACK READ -- centre = 2-scale, depth = 1-scale, period = 4 (q = 1), fb = 1/2. The triangle lands the
    //    delay on WHOLE samples at every offset, so each read is an exact integer FEEDBACK tap OFF THE LIVE BUFFER:
    //      k:        0  1  2  3  4  5
    //      delay(k): 2  3  2  1  2  3   (centre, top, centre, bottom, centre, top)
    //    On an impulse [8000,0,0,0,0,0], y[i] = dry[i] + 1/2-buf[i − delay(i)] reading already-written outputs:
    //      i0 d2: 8000 + 1/2-buf[-2]=0     = 8000
    //      i1 d3: 0    + 1/2-buf[-2]=0     = 0
    //      i2 d2: 0    + 1/2-buf[0]=8000   = 4000
    //      i3 d1: 0    + 1/2-buf[2]=4000   = 2000   (feedback: reads the output just written at i2)
    //      i4 d2: 0    + 1/2-buf[2]=4000   = 2000
    //      i5 d3: 0    + 1/2-buf[2]=4000   = 2000
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 8000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 1, 2);
        const want = [_]i16{ 8000, 0, 4000, 2000, 2000, 2000 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.ModulatedFeedbackWrong;
    }

    // 4. A LOUD CONSTRUCTIVE SUM SATURATES RATHER THAN WRAPS -- a loud steady signal under a loud in-phase feedback read exceeds the i16
    //    rail; the sum pins to sample_max (ALES3's ceiling) instead of wrapping negative. A steady 30000, centre 1-scale depth 0 (a fixed
    //    whole delay of 1), fb 1/1 is refused (runaway), so fb 1/2: index 1 on = 30000 + 1/2-30000 = 45000, saturated to 32767.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000 });
        try flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1 * scale, 0, 4, 1, 2);
        // index 0: dry 30000 (near reaches before the clip, silence); index 1..3: 30000 + 15000 = 45000 -> 32767.
        const want = [_]i16{ 30000, 32767, 32767, 32767 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.SaturateWrong;
    }

    // 5a. A PERIOD not a positive multiple of four refuses BadPeriod; zero refuses BadPeriod; a period wider than max_clip refuses BadPeriod.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2 * scale, 1 * scale, 6, 1, 2)) |_| return error.OddPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongOddPeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedOddPeriodMutated;
        if (flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2 * scale, 1 * scale, 0, 1, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongZeroPeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedZeroPeriodMutated;
        const wide: u32 = timeline.max_clip + 4;
        if (flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2 * scale, 1 * scale, wide, 1, 2)) |_| return error.WidePeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongWidePeriod;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedWidePeriodMutated;
    }

    // 5b. A DEPTH that swings below one whole sample refuses BadDepth; a swing top past max_clip refuses BadDelay -- each before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        // centre 1-scale, depth 1-scale -> bottom = 0 < glide_scale
        if (flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 1, 2)) |_| return error.LowSwingSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDepth) return error.WrongLowSwing;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedLowSwingMutated;
        // top = centre + depth whole past max_clip
        const wide_top: i64 = (@as(i64, timeline.max_clip) + 1) * scale;
        if (flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, wide_top, 2 * scale, 4, 1, 2)) |_| return error.WideTopSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDelay) return error.WrongWideTop;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedWideTopMutated;
    }

    // 5c. A FEEDBACK at unity (numerator == denominator, a runaway) refuses BadFeedback; a zero denominator refuses BadFeedback -- each
    //     before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 4, 4)) |_| return error.UnityFbSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFeedback) return error.WrongUnityFb;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedUnityFbMutated;
        if (flanger(&clip, 0, clip.len, 2 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 1, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFeedback) return error.WrongZeroDen;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedZeroDenMutated;
    }

    // 5d. AN OUT-OF-RANGE SPAN refuses BadRange before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (flanger(&clip, 2, 5, 2 * scale, 1 * scale, 4, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-flanger: the FLANGER — a short modulated delay with a FEEDBACK path, the family's first rung that adds a genuinely " ++
        "new element rather than only changing the mix. It reuses ALES71's triangle_delay_at driver and ALES70's fractional interpolation, " ++
        "yet reads them OFF THE LIVE BUFFER (ALES66's feedback — the delay line is the clip buffer itself) rather than a frozen snapshot: " ++
        "because the delay is at least one whole sample both neighbours sit strictly before i, so the read hears prior OUTPUT (feedback) " ++
        "safely in place. y[i] = saturate(x[i] + fb·interp(buf, i, d_s(i))). fb = 0 is the dry identity; a ZERO-DEPTH flanger is EXACTLY " ++
        "ALES66's echo at its centre byte-for-byte (the periodic driver reduces to the fixed one at rest); a hand-computed modulated-" ++
        "feedback read matches exactly (reading the outputs just written); a loud constructive sum SATURATES to the i16 rail rather than " ++
        "wrapping; and a period not a positive multiple of four refuses BadPeriod, a swing below one whole sample BadDepth, a swing top " ++
        "past max_clip BadDelay, a feedback at unity or a zero denominator BadFeedback (a runaway can never begin), an out-of-range span " ++
        "BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate. The " ++
        "reverb (many delays and feedbacks composed into a dense tail) is the last and largest rung of the time-based wing\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
